Introduction

This final project requires you to apply the core skills learned in this course, specifically process mapping, process narrative development, and reflective evaluation. Using the provided use case, you will create a detailed process map that visually represents the workflow, develop a structured narrative that explains each step with clarity and accuracy, and write a thoughtful reflection that describes what you learned, the challenges you faced, and how process documentation can be validated and improved in real organizational settings.

The project is designed to strengthen your ability to translate operational scenarios into clear, complete, and professional process documentation. By completing this final project, you will be able to:

Analyze a real-world operational scenario and translate it into a detailed process map that includes actors, flows, decisions, and alternative paths.
Construct a structured, professional process narrative that clearly explains each step of the workflow, encompassing triggers, inputs, outputs, actors, decisions, and exceptions.
Evaluate and reflect on your own process documentation, identifying strengths, challenges, and opportunities for improvement in both mapping and narrative-building activities.

Use case: Request defective part replacement
Scenario: The scenario describes a customer who returns to an auto parts store because a recently purchased part is defective. The store must verify the purchase, inspect the part, check inventory, and decide whether to issue a replacement, refund, credit, or deny the request. Your task is to document this entire process clearly through a process map and a process narrative.

Actor: Customer (Auto Parts Buyer)
Goal: Replace a defective auto part recently purchased.




